preConditionHook:
// NEAR-ZERO GIVE-BACK INSIDE A SPIKE. The bar's low never fell more than
// toleranceTicks below the PRIOR bar's close; mirrored, its high never rose
// more than that above the prior close. Brooks's "the lows of the bars are at
// or just one tick below the prior bar's close".
//
// ONE-SIDED, NOT A BAND. R02-07 adds a lower bound (the bar must actually have
// dipped, low[0] <= close[1]) and wraps the whole thing in an existence scan
// over a ten-bar spike window; V00-45's plain single-bar form wins, because the
// lower bound EXCLUDES the strongest case of all -- a bar whose low never
// trades back into the prior bar -- which is the opposite of what the sign is
// for.
//
// ONE RULE, TWO MERGED KEYS. LowNearPriorClose (same slice, sources V00-37 and
// T19-04) is this identical expression with the input spelled overlapTicks and
// the same default of 1, so it is not authored; both books' readings are the
// same test. T19-04's extra low[0] <= close[1] term is the same mining artifact
// R02-07 has, and is dropped for the same reason.
//
// Not CloseNearPriorLow, which compares this bar's close against the PRIOR
// bar's low plus an offset in price points -- a different pair of operands in
// the opposite sense.
//
// The tick is written division-first, mirroring EL's MinMove / PriceScale, and
// read once per bar into a local since both are per-symbol constants. Every
// scratch value is a localVariables entry, never a hook-scope declaration, so
// the hooks survive a double placement. Deepest bar touched: 1.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
